course: dictionary
lesson: 334
title: Ankle: the joint that moves your foot
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Your ankle is the joint where your leg meets your foot. It lets your foot bend up and down and turn from side to side, so you can walk, run and jump.
explain: Read the words around «ankle» to work out what it means. «Maddie twists her ankle in a scrap behind the garages, so it swells to twice its size.» Twisting and swelling are things that happen to a body part, and the sentence shows that the ankle is the part that got hurt.
explain: Some words tell you more about the ankle. «A rolled ankle stretches or tears the ligaments, the tough straps that hold the joint together.» The sentence explains what ligaments are, so you can check: the straps hold the joint together, and they are what gets hurt when an ankle rolls.
fact: The ankle is a joint where the bones of your leg meet a bone in your foot.
fact: Strong straps called ligaments hold the ankle together.
fact: «Ankle» appears in phrases such as «ankle socks», «ankle boots» and «ankle-deep».
fact: «Sprain» means to twist or stretch a joint, and a sprained ankle can hurt a lot and swell.
fact: For a sprained ankle, rest it, put a cold pack wrapped in a cloth on it, support it gently, raise the foot, and tell an adult.
exercise: What does the ankle let your foot do? | Grow new toenails | Store food | Hear sounds | *Bend up and down and turn
exercise: «A rolled ankle stretches or tears the ligaments, the tough straps that hold the joint together.» What are ligaments? | Socks for running | *The tough straps that hold the joint together | Soft bones in the foot | Parts of the mouth
exercise: «Maddie twists her ankle and tells no one.» The doctor says what she should have had. What is it? | To hide the swelling | A new pair of ankle boots | Ten more minutes of running | *Ice and a check-up the same day
test: Which phrase means the water came up to your ankles? | twist the knee | ankle socks | *ankle-deep | a broken arm
test: «A boy in Texas jumped off a high garage roof into a pile of leaves, thinking they were soft.» Why was that dangerous? | The leaves were too pretty to land on | He landed in water and got wet | Leaves are always soft enough to stop a fall | *The leaves were thin, and the hard ground underneath broke both his ankles
exam: For a sprained ankle, what is the best first step? | Run on it to see if it works | Ignore it until it stops hurting | *Rest it, put ice on it and raise it, then tell an adult | Wrap it in a hot blanket and forget it
